    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p7_b21",
      "text": "Calibration study.\nCalibration is important for down-\nstream safety, abstention, and tool use. We evaluate ex-\npected calibration error (ECE) and Brier score using node-\nlevel verifier probabilities and judge confidence signals,\nwhere available.\nTable 9 reports averages across tasks\nfor SFT, DPO, and TUR-DPO. TUR-DPO consistently re-\nduces both ECE and Brier score, indicating that uncertainty-\nweighted updates avoid overconfident learning from brittle\npairs. Reliability diagrams (Appendix Figure 4) show re-\nduced miscalibration in high-confidence bins.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.08,
        412.27,
        543.1,
        530.0
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1228,
        1649,
        2172,
        2119
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 558,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00224_p7_b22",
      "text": "We additionally report ECE with 95% bootstrap confidence\nintervals (10k resamples, stratified by task) and decompose\ncalibration by confidence bin. As shown in Table 10, TUR-\nDPO improves calibration across all bins, with the largest\ngains in the highest-confidence regime. This behavior is\nconsistent with uncertainty weighting suppressing large gra-\ndient updates on unstable pairs. Under prompt perturbations\nthat introduce benign distractors, TUR-DPO maintains ECE\nwithin 0.01 of the base condition, whereas DPO degrades by\n0.02, indicating greater robustness to surface-level variation.\n",
      "bbox": [
        306.97,
        538.01,
        543.19,
        655.5
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1227,
        2152,
        2172,
